<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n1380" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Aesōpus</span></b> , i, m.,
		= <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Αἴσωπος</span>. <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<i>Æsop</i>, <i>the Greek
				fabulist of Phrygia</i>, <i>in the time of
				Crœsus;</i> cf. <i><span class="en">Phaedr. 5</span></i> prol. The difference  between Æsopic and Libystic fables, v.
			under Aesopicus.—Cf. <i><span class="en">Quint. 5,
				11, 19</span></i>; <i><span class="en">Gell. 2,
			29</span></i>.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<i>A tragic actor</i>, <i>friend of
				Cicero</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">noster Aesopus,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Cic.
					Fam. 7, 1</span></i>
			; <i><span class="en">Hor. Ep. 2, 1, 82</span></i>; cf. <i><span class="en">Cic. Tusc. 4,
				25</span></i>; <i><span class="en">id. Div. 1, 37</span></i>.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
